        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.752,
            0.046,
            0.862,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "Number Play"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.087,
            0.849,
            0.146
          ],
          "content": "In general, **if a divides M and a divides N, then a divides M + N and a divides M – N. In other words, if M and N are multiples of a, then M + N and M – N will also be multiples of a.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.152,
            0.848,
            0.194
          ],
          "content": "？ 2. If a number is divisible by 8, then 8 also divides any two numbers (separately) that add up to the number."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.201,
            0.886,
            0.463
          ],
          "content": "<table><tr><td colspan=3>Explanation with Algebra and Visualisation</td><td>Examples</td></tr><tr><td>A numberdivisible by 8 isa multiple of 8.</td><td>8m</td><td rowspan=2>nrows  u(a+b) rows,            <img src=\"images/page_7_table_image_4_1.jpg\"/>b rowsoooo</td><td rowspan=2>8, 16, 56, 72.72 = 48 + 24(8×98×6+8×3).72 = 50 + 22</td></tr><tr><td>A numberdivisibleby 8 can beexpressed asa sum of twomultiples of 8 orsum of two non-multiples of 8.</td><td> $8m = 8a + 8b$ 8m = p + q(p, q notmultiples of8)</td></tr></table>",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.149,
                0.467,
                0.485,
                0.486
              ],
              "content": "So, statement 2 is sometimes true."
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_table_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.493,
            0.847,
            0.535
          ],
          "content": "3. If a number is divisible by 7, then all multiples of that number will be divisible by 7."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.541,
            0.889,
            0.852
          ],
          "content": "<table><tr><td colspan=\"4\">Explanation with Algebra and Visualisation Examples</td></tr><tr><td>Numbers divisible by 7 will have 7 as a factor.</td><td colspan=\"2\">7j</td><td> $14 = 7 \\times 2 (j = 2).$   $42 = 7 \\times 6 (j = 6).$   $98 = 7 \\times 14 (j = 14).$ </td></tr><tr><td>This contains a total of mj rows. So this is also a multiple of 7.</td><td>(7j)×m</td><td><img src=\"images/page_7_table_image_7_1.jpg\"/> m times <img src=\"images/page_7_table_image_7_2.jpg\"/></td><td>Some multiples of 14:  $2 8 = ( 7 \\times 2 ) \\times 2 .$   $7 0 = ( 7 \\times 2 ) \\times 5 .$   $1 5 4 = ( 7 \\times 2 ) \\times 1 1$ </td></tr></table>",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_table_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.862,
            0.848,
            0.901
          ],
          "content": "The number 7jm or $( 7   \\times   j   \\times   m )$ has a factor of 7. We can see that Statement 3 is always true."
        },
